I'm sending out a LOT of copies of this by mail, so I guess I ought to just
byte the bullet and post it again. 

This program acts as a front end to the tar program, giving it the added
functionality of allowing multiple tapes and incremental backups of
directory structures. It was originally written on a 4.1 binary system (so
I couldn't hack tar) and is known to work on 4.1 and 4.2 systems. 

One current problem is that it tends to be a resource hog on large
directory structures because of the requirement to make repeated 'tar r'
calls to fit filenames on to a command line with exec(). If you modify tar
to accept filenames from stdin, you can convert that hack into a pipe and
clean things up considerably. 

There are a few other brain damages outstanding, most of them caused by
Unix and the inability to intelligently handle things like tape errors and
end of tape. Because of the latter, there is a compiled in constant (I
know, it should be run time changeable...) that estimates the amount of
data you can shove onto a tape, and when you exceed it, you switch tapes.
it works, but I don't like it. 

Good luck.

chuq
